WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.640
Peace be upon you. Welcome to the final video of the second module in our fellowship.

00:05.060 --> 00:08.320
Our meeting today is not just a lesson we add to our information and finish;

00:08.580 --> 00:14.600
this is a comprehensive intellectual upgrade to our mentality and way of thinking as professionals and health leaders.

00:14.860 --> 00:23.180
Any ordinary person might look at the spread of a certain disease in a neighborhood and say: "Oh Protector, this is bad luck or some bacteria roaming in the air entertaining itself on people."

00:23.180 --> 00:26.160
Yet we are here to change that perspective completely:

00:26.160 --> 00:31.620
We want to understand how a public health researcher and leader sees the world through different lenses,

00:31.780 --> 00:37.760
and deconstruct complex phenomena with scientific, professional tools that dismantle what lies behind the superficial scene.

00:37.900 --> 00:44.300
To accomplish this mental upgrade, our roadmap today begins with the mentality of the public health researcher:

00:44.460 --> 00:47.220
Public health science does not rely on a single narrow lens;

00:47.640 --> 00:51.180
it rests upon an ecosystem of robust theoretical frameworks and models,

00:51.340 --> 00:53.240
as if assembling a large jigsaw puzzle:

00:53.560 --> 00:54.820
A lens revealing the physical body,

00:54.820 --> 00:56.520
a lens examining the psychological state,

00:56.820 --> 00:58.320
a lens observing society,

00:58.600 --> 01:02.380
and a lens studying environment, policies, and laws.

01:02.620 --> 01:05.000
These models are not ink on paper in drawers;

01:05.000 --> 01:12.000
they are compasses that guide our hands in the field.

01:12.000 --> 01:18.000
In this video, we will review the five core conceptual frameworks that summarize public health.

01:18.000 --> 01:25.000
The first station: The biopsychosocial model: Breaking the monopoly of pure biology.

01:25.000 --> 01:33.000
For centuries, classical medicine viewed the human body as an engine: If a part breaks, we fix it with pills or surgery.

01:33.000 --> 01:42.000
Yet George Engel came and said: "No, a human being is not an engine; health is a triangular interaction: biology, psychology, and society."

01:42.000 --> 01:50.000
Biology is the genetic code, bacteria, and blood pressure.

01:50.000 --> 01:58.000
Psychology is anxiety, psychological distress, fear, and feeling depressed.

01:58.000 --> 02:08.000
And society is the family, employment, social security, and financial pressures.

02:08.000 --> 02:18.000
These three vertices constantly interact: Depression raises stress hormones and damages immunity, while social isolation worsens heart attacks.

02:18.000 --> 02:26.000
We cannot treat a heart patient without addressing the financial stress and grief suffocating them.

02:26.000 --> 02:35.000
Now we move to the second station: The Social Ecological Model (SEM): The circles of influence.

02:35.000 --> 02:44.000
The individual does not live isolated; they are surrounded by five concentric circles:

02:44.000 --> 02:52.000
The individual circle: knowledge, biological age, and personal beliefs.

02:52.000 --> 03:00.000
The interpersonal circle: family, friends, and peers who influence daily habits.

03:00.000 --> 03:10.000
The organizational circle: schools, workplaces, and places of worship that set daily schedules and food offerings.

03:10.000 --> 03:20.000
The community circle: the physical neighborhood, streets, sidewalks, and availability of clean water and fresh markets.

03:20.000 --> 03:30.000
And the public policy circle: laws, government regulations, municipal enforcement, and climate policies.

03:30.000 --> 03:39.260
If you want to change behavior, you cannot focus only on the individual while the surrounding four circles pull them back.

03:39.260 --> 03:42.540
True change touches all five circles together.

03:42.540 --> 03:47.340
Now we enter the third station: Determinants and levels of prevention.

03:47.440 --> 03:50.540
The Social Determinants of Health (SDOH) model reminds us

03:50.840 --> 03:57.940
of the shocking reality: Human health is determined by the details of daily living far more than by operating rooms.

03:58.260 --> 04:02.180
Unjust distribution of resources is the factory of health disparities.

04:02.600 --> 04:08.960
In climate crises: The person with financial stability lives in a thermally insulated home, pays the electricity bill for air conditioning,

04:08.960 --> 04:13.960
and drinks filtered water at peace. While the person living under a tin roof in an informal settlement

04:14.640 --> 04:18.560
cannot afford a fan, and their water comes from a tanker that could be contaminated at the first rain.

04:18.560 --> 04:23.540
The health educator knows that their health battle cannot be separated from social justice.

04:23.640 --> 04:26.860
Here comes the prevention pyramid: When do we intervene?

04:26.860 --> 04:32.660
As we explained in previous videos: Primary prevention: We uproot the danger from its origin before it nears the body:

04:32.660 --> 04:39.240
Greening neighborhoods, painting house roofs reflective white, and setting early warnings three days before a heatwave.

04:39.400 --> 04:44.040
Secondary prevention: Here we race against time to detect danger at its earliest moments:

04:44.040 --> 04:49.340
Screening dizziness and headache symptoms in workers, cooling them down before they fall into a heatstroke coma,

04:49.420 --> 04:52.180
and checking children's lungs as soon as a dust storm blows.

04:52.340 --> 04:57.360
And tertiary prevention: When catastrophe occurs, we intervene in the hospital to prevent death,

04:57.360 --> 05:00.740
alleviate kidney and heart failure complications with rehabilitation.

05:01.000 --> 05:04.380
Our greatest arena as educators is the primary and secondary levels:

05:04.680 --> 05:07.860
Building bastions of safety in the neighborhood before they reach the intensive care unit.

05:07.860 --> 05:12.640
In the fourth station, we widen the lens to the maximum degree to encompass the entire planet:

05:13.000 --> 05:14.540
The One Health framework.

05:14.700 --> 05:21.540
This model proves that human health, animal health, and environmental health are a single umbilical cord and an indivisible fabric.

05:21.940 --> 05:25.860
More than 60 percent of serious infectious diseases are zoonotic in origin;

05:26.400 --> 05:31.840
they jumped to us due to forest deforestation, haphazard expansion, and wildlife trafficking.

05:31.840 --> 05:37.320
When climate alters temperatures and precipitation, mosquitoes, ticks, and sandflies

05:37.320 --> 05:42.840
expand their habitats, transmitting dengue, malaria, and leishmaniasis to completely new areas.

05:43.320 --> 05:49.300
We cannot protect a child from dengue fever with mere advice while swamps fill the neighborhood and the environment is destroyed.

05:49.580 --> 05:52.040
Human health begins with protecting the planet.

05:52.120 --> 05:55.980
In the fifth station, we zoom in on the engineering of change:

05:56.100 --> 05:59.940
Health behavior change models, such as the Health Belief Model (HBM).

05:59.940 --> 06:03.080
Beware of falling into the trap of thinking information alone is enough for change:

06:03.080 --> 06:06.780
No matter how much we reiterate this in all videos, we must always remember it:

06:07.180 --> 06:12.580
All smokers know that smoking causes cancer, and all people exposed to the sun know it is hot.

06:13.040 --> 06:18.940
Behavior requires perceived threat: feeling that danger touches them personally and harms their children;

06:19.160 --> 06:24.380
self-efficacy: trusting their ability to implement the behavior, that it is neither complicated nor costly;

06:24.380 --> 06:29.580
and breaking barriers: guiding them to cheap alternatives from their kitchen instead of demanding expensive solutions.

06:29.580 --> 06:37.100
Now we reach the summit: Integrative diagnosis: Assembling the puzzle pieces before the crisis of construction workers and sunstrokes:

06:37.460 --> 06:44.400
Here we open the biopsychosocial lens to observe dehydration physiology alongside the worker's fear of losing their daily wage and poverty.

06:44.800 --> 06:51.240
We expand with SEM to view peer pressure at the site, the contractor's greed, and the absence of municipal inspection.

06:51.480 --> 06:57.700
We apply SDOH to comprehend that he only risked his life to bring a bite of bread to his children.

06:57.700 --> 06:59.560
We then summon the prevention pyramid:

06:59.580 --> 07:07.240
Rejecting mere reliance on ambulances (tertiary), and demanding mandatory shade canopies and rest intervals (primary).

07:07.700 --> 07:14.400
We connect with One Health to see how the absence of trees and excess asphalt created heat islands suffocating the site.

07:14.840 --> 07:20.900
And we utilize behavioral models to train workers to monitor dizziness symptoms and drink water without embarrassment.

07:21.060 --> 07:25.260
This is how a public health leader thinks with a 360-degree vision.

07:25.260 --> 07:28.640
The conclusion: Public health is not scattered pieces of advice;

07:28.640 --> 07:37.620
it is a comprehensive thinking system: We do not treat superficial symptoms; we dismantle the interaction of body with mind, society, resource justice, and climate.

07:37.960 --> 07:45.640
With this robust mindset, we have officially concluded the entire second module. You now possess the solid intellectual foundation to be true leaders.

07:46.500 --> 07:54.780
We leave you with an applied activity: Think of the rise in pediatric asthma attacks during dust storms and heatwaves in your neighborhood:

07:54.780 --> 08:04.880
How do you apply the SEM model at the family, school, and policy levels? What action represents primary prevention and what action represents secondary prevention?

08:05.080 --> 08:11.780
And how do you utilize the One Health principle to explain the increase in dust storms and their link to overgrazing and vegetation degradation?

08:12.240 --> 08:18.580
Thank you for your attention, congratulations on finishing the second module, and we will see you in the third module with full determination and passion.
